NAME
    bp - manage execution breakpoints, data breakpoints, and logpoints

SYNOPSIS
    bp add <addr>[-<end>] [if <expr>] [log <expr>]
    bp write <space> <addr>[-<end>] [<value>] [changed] [if <expr>] [log <expr>]
    bp read <space> <addr>[-<end>] [<value>] [if <expr>] [log <expr>]
    bp uninit <space>
    bp when [<condition>] [log|off]
    bp depth <n>|off
    bp forbid <addr>-<end>
    bp log [<count>] | bp log clear
    bp list
    bp on|off <id>
    bp remove <id>
    bp <cpu> add|list|remove ...

DESCRIPTION
    Manages execution breakpoints, memory read/write breakpoints,
    uninitialized-read traps, hardware condition traps, call-depth guards,
    suppression ranges, and non-halting logpoints for the attached
    session. Named 'bp' to avoid shadowing the shell's break(1) keyword.

    All addresses and ranges are parsed as hexadecimal (optional '$' or
    '0x' prefix; symbolic labels from label(1) are accepted). Passing
    '<start>-<end>' matches any address in the inclusive range. Breakpoint
    IDs also appear in ps(1) as 'bp<id>' and can be removed with kill(1).

    Prefixing a processor name from cpus(1) targets that processor's own
    breakpoint list and address space (for example, 'bp sa1 add 0082D7' or
    'bp gsu add 0A80E9'). Processors that cannot halt refuse breakpoints.

OPTIONS
    add <addr>[-<end>] [if <expr>] [log <expr>]
        Halt just before executing <addr> (or any address in the range).
        With 'log <expr>', record evaluated expressions and continue
        without halting.

    write <space> <addr>[-<end>] [<value>] [changed] [if <expr>] [log <expr>]
        Halt (or log) when <addr> in <space> is written, optionally only
        when the written byte equals hex <value> and/or differs from the
        last byte observed by this breakpoint ('changed'). The first write
        seen always counts as changed. Halts land on the instruction after
        the store; data logpoints record at the access itself.

    read <space> <addr>[-<end>] [<value>] [if <expr>] [log <expr>]
        Halt (or log) when <addr> in <space> is read, optionally gated on
        hex byte <value>. On cores reporting read hooks before execution,
        halts land before the load instruction executes; cores without
        read-hook support accept 'bp read' without firing.

    uninit <space>
        Halt the first time any byte of <space> is read before anything
        has written to it since arming. Each address halts once and is
        then marked initialized. One space may be armed at a time.

    when [<condition>] [log|off]
        List, arm, log, or disarm core-defined hardware defect conditions.
        When a core publishes no hardware conditions, exits with status 1.

    depth <n> | depth off
        Halt once (and auto-disarm) if the call stack depth exceeds <n>,
        or cancel the depth guard with 'off'.

    forbid <addr>-<end>
        Suppress all address, data, and uninitialized-read halts while PC
        is inside <addr>-<end>. Explicit step(1) operations remain active
        inside forbidden ranges.

    log [<count>] | log clear
        Show the last <count> logpoint entries (default 32, ring capacity
        4096 per processor), or clear the log ring.

    list
        List active breakpoints and forbid ranges with IDs, resolved
        labels, enabled state, and hit counts.

    on <id> | off <id> | remove <id>
        Enable, disable (preserving configuration and hit count), or
        delete breakpoint #<id>.

CONDITIONS AND LOGPOINTS
    'if <expr>' evaluates <expr> using the eval(1) language against live
    registers and memory at the matching processor scope before halting.
    Syntax and symbol names are validated when the breakpoint is added;
    invalid expressions fail with status 1 without adding a breakpoint. A
    runtime evaluation error (such as division by zero) halts once and
    drops the condition while keeping the breakpoint. Quote expressions
    containing shell operators ('&&', '||', '<', '>', '|').

    'log <expr>' accepts a comma-separated list of expressions, records
    each as 'expr=value' into the per-processor log ring, and continues
    execution without halting. 'if' and 'log' compose: the condition gates
    whether the logpoint records.

EXAMPLES
    bp add 8000
    bp add 80A31C if x == 7
    bp add 80A31C log "a, x"
    bp write WRAM 0DB3 log "a, [$7E0100]" if "a > 4"
    bp log 100
    bp add 818000-818FFF
    bp add 808000 if "[$7E0020] != 0 && a > 16"
    bp write VRAM 2760
    bp write WRAM 0100-01FF
    bp write WRAM 0DB3 changed
    bp read WRAM 13c6
    bp uninit WRAM
    bp depth 40
    bp forbid 0080C4-0080FF
    bp off 1
    bp list
    bp sa1 add 0082D7
    bp gsu add 0A80E9 if "r14 > $100"

EXIT STATUS
    0   Command completed (or usage/non-fatal refusal printed).
    1   Invalid condition or logpoint expression, unsupported hardware
        conditions, unsized space, no target attached, or session fault.

SEE ALSO
